# Line-ending normalisation.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# ---------------
# Every text blob in this repository is stored LF-only (verified: 612 of 612
# tracked text files). Nothing in the repo declared that, so each clone was
# left to guess from its own `core.autocrlf` setting. On a Windows clone with
# autocrlf unset, git compared CRLF working-tree bytes against LF blobs and
# reported 533 modified files, 194,476 insertions and 194,476 deletions, with
# zero real content changes. That diff is indistinguishable at a glance from
# catastrophic damage, and committing it would have rewritten the entire
# repository's line endings for everyone.
#
# `text=auto` moves that decision out of each developer's local config and into
# the repository, where it belongs: text is normalised to LF on commit and
# checked out in the platform's native ending. This is exactly the behaviour
# the affected clone already gets from `core.autocrlf=true`, so it changes
# nothing for existing checkouts -- `git add --renormalize .` produces no diff.
# It prevents the next clone from starting in the broken state.
* text=auto

# Binary formats. `text=auto` detects these correctly on its own (it looks for
# NUL bytes), but stating them is cheaper than relying on a heuristic, and it
# documents that 93.7% of this repo by weight is binary -- see backlog item
# C19.1, which proposes moving the bundled wheels out of the tree entirely.
*.whl   binary
*.gz    binary
*.mkv   binary
*.png   binary
*.PNG   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.db    binary

# Shell scripts run under bash on Windows too: keep them LF whatever core.autocrlf says.
*.sh text eol=lf
